  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a relatively small, cross-sectional survey of 221 respondents, which limits the ability to establish causal relationships between green space quality and loneliness.",
      "what": "This study analyzed survey data from 221 respondents in India to examine how subjective perceptions of urban green spaces relate to loneliness, measured via the UCLA Loneliness Scale. Regression and correlation analyses revealed that negative perceptions of green space accessibility, safety, and design are significantly associated with higher loneliness, particularly for women and younger demographics. Specific design features like shaded seating and recreational facilities showed stronger associations with reduced loneliness than simple physical proximity.",
      "why": "The findings shift the focus of urban greening from mere physical availability to qualitative and user-sensitive design, demonstrating that how people experience and feel safe in a space is more critical for mitigating social isolation than how close they live to it."
    }
  },
